{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And I buried her there</b> — It is written in Rashi's commentary: \"and I did not even bring her to Bethlehem to bring her into the land.\" But I do not know what he means — for was she, Heaven forbid, buried outside the land? She died in the land and there she was buried, as it says here in this portion, \"Rachel died on me in the land of Canaan,\" and there it is further written explicitly (Genesis 35:6), \"And Jacob came to Luz, which is in the land of Canaan — that is, Bethel,\" and it is written (ibid. v. 16), \"And they journeyed from Bethel, and there was still some distance of land to come to Ephrath,\" and she died on the road between Bethel and Bethlehem Ephrathah, in the land of Israel." "<b>And I buried her there.</b> \"And I know that there is resentment in your heart toward me, but know that by decree of the Divine Word I buried her there, so that she might be a help to her children when Nebuzaradan exiles them and they pass by there — she would emerge from her grave and seek mercy for them, as it is said (Jeremiah 31:14), 'A voice is heard in Ramah, Rachel weeping for her children,' and the Holy One, blessed be He, answers her, 'There is reward for your labor… and the children shall return to their own border'\" — this is Rashi's language (Rashi on Genesis 48:7). Now in any event there needs to be a hint in the verse for the reason stated in this Aggadah. And perhaps this is what the verse means when it says, <b>Rachel died on me on the road, and I buried her there on the road</b> — that is, on the road through which her children would pass, there she died, and there I buried her for their benefit. For she did not die on the road, but in Ramah, which is a city in the territory of Benjamin, and there she was buried; rather, she died on the road of the future, and the verse does not spell out future events but only alludes to them.\n\nAnd according to the plain sense too, he said this to him by way of excuse, so that Joseph would not feel pained upon seeing his wish [fulfilled regarding] burial in the Cave of Machpelah — that he did not bury his mother there, especially since he buried Leah there. Therefore he told him that she died in the land of Canaan and was not buried outside the land, as Jacob's burial would be in Egypt; and she died on the road suddenly and unexpectedly, and he could not bury her there — for how could he abandon his sons and his livestock on the road and go quickly with her to the Cave of Machpelah? And where would he find physicians and remedies to embalm her? And this is the meaning of \"on me.\" And even though the Cave of Machpelah is not far from there — only about half a day's journey — Jacob was heavily encumbered with great livestock and household, and they would not arrive there except after many days. And indeed he spent many days on that road until he came to his father; and the Sages state (Mo'ed Katan 27) that [the funeral arrangements] were not like those of women, forever, on account of [maintaining] dignity.\n\nNow I am inclined to think that these were words of excuse, and Joseph too knew that she had died on the road and was buried in the land, and that [Jacob] treated her with honor in her death. But Jacob's intention was that he did not bring her to the Cave so that he would not bury two sisters there, for he would be ashamed before his fathers: Leah was the one married to him first with proper license, and Rachel — out of his love for her, with the vow that he had vowed to her — he took her."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 48, :notes {1 "In the passage from Rashi, 'על פי הדבור' is rendered as 'by decree of the Divine Word'; the phrase could also mean 'by divine command' more generally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:46:31.372222567Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}